name: pr-decomposition
description: "Use this skill whenever implementing any feature, endpoint, or change in the FuelGuard backend. Decomposes work into atomic, stackable PRs suitable for Graphite (gt) stacking. Triggers: any new feature, endpoint addition, schema change, refactor, or bug fix. Prevents oversized PRs. Enforces one logical concern per PR."
---

# PR Decomposition — FuelGuard Backend

## Core Principle

Every unit of work MUST be decomposed into the smallest independently-reviewable, independently-deployable change. No PR should do more than one logical thing.

---

## Atomicity Rules

A PR is atomic when ALL of the following are true:

1. It has exactly one purpose (add, change, or fix one thing)
2. It can be reviewed without understanding other in-flight PRs
3. It does not break tests when merged alone (or is explicitly a WIP stacked PR)
4. It touches the minimum number of files needed for its purpose
5. Its title fits in one sentence without "and"

If a PR title contains "and", it MUST be split.

---

## Complexity Thresholds

| Lines Changed | PR Status | Action Required |
|---|---|---|
| < 200 lines | ✅ Acceptable | Proceed |
| 200–400 lines | ⚠️ Review | Justify in PR description or split |
| 400–600 lines | 🔴 Large | MUST split unless it is purely generated code (e.g. migrations, swagger) |
| > 600 lines | ❌ Blocked | Always split. No exceptions. |

Generated/mechanical code (schema migrations, OpenAPI JSON, test fixtures) does not count toward the 600-line limit but must be in its own PR.

---

## Standard Decomposition Patterns

### Pattern: New API Endpoint

Split into this exact stack order:

```
PR 1: DB migration — new table or column only
PR 2: DB query functions — repository layer only, no routes
PR 3: Service logic — business rules, no HTTP layer
PR 4: Route + controller — wires HTTP to service, minimal logic
PR 5: Swagger/OpenAPI — documents the new endpoint
PR 6: Tests — unit + integration + smoke tests for the feature
```

Each PR stacks on the previous. Do not combine any two layers.

### Pattern: Alert Rule Addition

```
PR 1: Threshold config — add new env var + config/thresholds.js entry
PR 2: Rule implementation — alertRuleEngine.js, rule function only
PR 3: Rule tests — unit tests for the rule
PR 4: Smoke test — end-to-end test that fires the alert
PR 5: Docs update — update docs/FuelGuard_Backend_Spec.md alert table
```

### Pattern: Schema Change

```
PR 1: Migration file only — SQL ALTER or CREATE TABLE
PR 2: Supabase client types update — TypeScript types or JSDoc
PR 3: Repository layer update — query changes only
PR 4: Service layer update — if logic changes
PR 5: Docs — update schema section in FuelGuard_Backend_Spec.md
```

### Pattern: WebSocket Event Addition

```
PR 1: Event type constant — add to shared constants file
PR 2: Broadcast function — websocketService.js
PR 3: Trigger wiring — wire into telemetry pipeline or job
PR 4: Frontend contract docs — document event shape in spec
PR 5: Tests — test that event fires correctly
```

### Pattern: Bug Fix

```
PR 1: Failing test that reproduces the bug (test MUST fail on main)
PR 2: Fix — minimal code change to make the test pass
```

Never fix a bug without a test first.

---

## Graphite (gt) Stack Commands

When implementing a stacked feature, use this workflow:

```bash
# Start from main
gt sync
gt branch create feat/fuelguard-<feature>-db-migration

# Do the work, commit
git add . && git commit -m "feat(db): add telemetry_normalized table migration"

# Stack next PR on top
gt branch create feat/fuelguard-<feature>-repository

# Continue stacking...
gt branch create feat/fuelguard-<feature>-service
gt branch create feat/fuelguard-<feature>-route
gt branch create feat/fuelguard-<feature>-swagger
gt branch create feat/fuelguard-<feature>-tests

# Submit the whole stack for review
gt submit --stack
```

---

## Branch Naming Convention

```
feat/<scope>-<short-description>       # new functionality
fix/<scope>-<short-description>        # bug fix
chore/<scope>-<short-description>      # maintenance, deps, config
docs/<scope>-<short-description>       # documentation only
test/<scope>-<short-description>       # tests only
refactor/<scope>-<short-description>   # no behaviour change
```

Scopes for FuelGuard: `db`, `telemetry`, `vehicles`, `devices`, `drivers`, `alerts`, `trips`, `ws`, `auth`, `config`, `docs`

Examples:
```
feat/telemetry-ingestion-route
feat/alerts-low-fuel-rule
feat/ws-vehicle-update-broadcast
fix/trips-engine-state-transition
docs/fuelguard-spec-alert-types
```

---

## Pre-Decomposition Checklist

Before writing any code, answer these questions:

- [ ] What tables does this touch? (each table change = own PR)
- [ ] What services does this touch? (each service = own PR)
- [ ] Does this add a new API endpoint? (use New Endpoint pattern above)
- [ ] Does this change existing API behavior? (document breaking vs non-breaking)
- [ ] Does this add a new alert rule? (use Alert Rule pattern above)
- [ ] Does this change the WebSocket protocol? (document event shape change)
- [ ] Does this require a migration? (migration = first PR in stack)

---

## Conflict with This Skill

If Antigravity proposes a PR that violates these rules, it MUST stop and present options:

```
⚠️  PR DECOMPOSITION CONFLICT

Proposed PR "feat/telemetry-full-pipeline" would:
  - Change 3 DB tables
  - Add 2 service functions
  - Add 1 new route
  - Add 8 tests

This violates the atomicity rule (multiple logical concerns).

OPTIONS:
  A) Split into 6 PRs following the New Endpoint pattern
  B) Scope to DB migration only, defer service/route to follow-up PRs
  C) Proceed as single PR (requires explicit override: type OVERRIDE)

Which option? (A/B/C or describe alternative):
```

Never proceed with a non-atomic PR without explicit user confirmation.